{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The Bank is defined as \"Wells Fargo Bank Minnesota, N.A., or it's agent\", so the term \"Bank\" may or may not include whatever affiliate or agent the named bank puts in its place. That matters because the lending, the trust and the collateral investment were in fact run by Wells Fargo Bank, N.A. rather than the named Minnesota entity.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.27}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The recital describes the Program as established for the Bank's \"retirement plan, trust and custody clients\", and could be argued to limit the operative appointment in Section 1 to accounts of that character. Copic is an insurance company, and the recital's enumeration invites an argument that the Program's ERISA/trust design controls the operative terms.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The recital says the Bank will make securities available to \"selected\" brokerage firms and other borrowing organizations, which can be read as a promise that the Bank screens borrowers. That reading cannot easily be squared with Section 8, under which the Participant assumes all risk of loss from Borrower defaults.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.19}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The recital states that the Program \"is designed to allocate securities loans equitably among the Participants\", which conflicts with Section 3, where the Bank owes only \"reasonable efforts\" on a queue basis and a single Borrower may be lent all of a Participant's lendable securities. A court would have to decide whether the recital's equitable-allocation premise controls or yields to the operative allocation clause.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "Section 1 asserts that the custody agreements \"authorize Participant to appoint an agent to lend securities from the Account\", but the 1999 Custody Agreement with Norwest Bank Minnesota in the record contains no such authorization and no lending provision. The premise of the appointment therefore cannot be squared with the custody instrument it incorporates by reference.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "Paragraph f lists permitted cash-collateral investments (repurchase agreements, master notes, treasuries and agencies, CDs and time deposits, bankers acceptances, commercial paper) and then adds the catch-all \"and other short term money market instruments, and mutual funds holding any of the previously mentioned securities\", leaving open whether longer-dated structured, asset-backed or corporate paper qualifies. The catch-all is further qualified by \"subject to policy and standard guidelines established from time to time by the Bank\", which the Bank may argue lets it enlarge the list unilaterally.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.56},
     {"issue": "Paragraph f's permitted-investment list and its \"safety of principal and liquidity\" mandate cannot be squared with the investment policies of the vehicle the collateral was actually placed in, the Wells Fargo Collateral Investment for Term Loans Trust, whose Confidential Memorandum authorizes mortgage-backed, asset-backed, 144A and corporate notes with maturities up to five years and a 190-day weighted average maturity. A court would have to decide which document defines the permissible use of the Participant's cash collateral.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.40}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The Bank promises only \"reasonable efforts to allocate loans on a security by security queue basis\", with neither \"reasonable efforts\" nor the operation of the \"queue\" defined or benchmarked anywhere in the Agreement. The following sentence, permitting one Borrower to be lent all of a Participant's securities, deepens rather than resolves the standard's indeterminacy.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The section gives the Participant the right to demand termination of any loan \"for any reason at any time\" and recites one- and three-business-day return periods, then takes it back with \"Bank will have a reasonable time ... to liquidate cash collateral investments prior to terminating the loan\", with \"reasonable time\" undefined. If the collateral investments are illiquid, that proviso may swallow the termination right entirely.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.42}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The Participant's entitlement runs to cash dividends, stock dividends, splits, rights, conversion privileges, tender and exchange offers \"and similar corporate actlons\", a catch-all whose reach beyond the enumerated items is undefined. Whether an event not on the list is a \"similar corporate action\" giving the Participant an as-if-unloaned entitlement is left to construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.21}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The Bank's liability disclaimer turns on receipt of notice \"on trade date from the proper party\", but \"the proper party\" is nowhere identified in this Agreement. Who may give a valid sale notice — the Participant, its investment manager, its fiduciary, or the authorized persons listed under the Custody Agreement — is left open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
   ]}
]}

