{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c011",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The required monthly report must include \"all loan activity, Borrowers to whom loans were made and income earned\", an enumeration that says nothing about the composition or valuation of the pooled cash-collateral investments made under Section 2(f). Whether \"all loan activity\" reaches the collateral portfolio is exactly what a Participant complaining of undisclosed collateral losses would litigate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The 40/60 split is taken \"on the net earnings from each loan\", but \"net earnings\" is undefined and the same clause says all cash collateral is pooled, so per-loan net earnings cannot be computed on its own terms. In particular it does not say whether losses on the pooled collateral investments are netted against earnings before the Bank takes its 40%.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.34}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c013",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The first sentence puts \"all risk of loss arising out of ... collateral investment loss\" on the Participant while the last sentence puts on the Bank \"the risk of loss arising from negligent and freudvlent operation of its Securities Lending Program\", and the two cannot both be given their full scope. \"Negligent and fraudulent\" is also ambiguous as to whether negligence alone suffices, and \"operation of its Securities Lending Program\" does not say whether collateral investment is part of that operation.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.58},
     {"issue": "Section 8's assignment of all collateral-investment loss to the Participant cannot be squared with Section 2(f), which commits the Bank to invest cash collateral only in the listed short-term instruments with safety of principal and liquidity as prime considerations. A court must decide whether Section 8 excuses losses caused by investments outside the Section 2(f) mandate.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.31}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c014",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The clause says that \"upon mutual agreement, cither party may waive all or part of the Notice period\", which is internally contradictory: a waiver requiring mutual agreement is not a unilateral right of either party. The sentence leaves it unclear whether one party can shorten the 60-day period and on what terms.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.23}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c015",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The clause states only that notice \"shall be directed and mailed\" to a Denver address, without saying when notice is effective, whether any other method suffices, or how the address is changed. That matters because Section 9 measures a 60-day termination period from written notice.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.15}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c016",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "Notice must go to \"Wells Fargo Bank Minnesota N.A.\" attention of a named individual, an entity and person whose continued existence the Agreement does not address, while the preamble's \"or it's agent\" and Section 13's successors clause contemplate that another Wells Fargo entity may act. Whether notice to the successor lending agent, Wells Fargo Bank, N.A., satisfies this clause is unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.12}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c017",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The headings are excluded from the construction of the Agreement, yet the sections are identified only by those garbled headings — \"1k.\", \"1d.\", a missing Section 7 and an unnumbered compensation paragraph folded under the Section 6 heading. Excluding headings leaves the boundary between Section 6 and the compensation paragraph, and the numbering of the risk-of-loss section, to be worked out from the text alone.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.10}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c018",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "This Agreement and \"all transactions hereunder\" are governed by Minnesota law, while the Custody Agreement between the same parties for the same Account chooses Colorado law and subjects the custodied securities to Colorado insurance statutes. Because Section 1 makes the lending appointment depend on the custody arrangement, which law governs the collateral-investment dispute is contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c019",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The clause binds \"successors and assigns\" but the preamble names Wells Fargo Bank Minnesota, N.A. \"or it's agent\", and the lending, trusteeship and collateral investment were carried on by Wells Fargo Bank, N.A. Read together, the two leave open whether and how the successor entity took on the Bank's obligations, and the clause neither permits nor restricts assignment.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c020",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The effective date is left blank — \"effective on the ___ day of January, 2001\" — so the instrument fixes no date on its own words. That blank sits against the preamble's \"as of the [ ]st day of January 2001\" and a signature block dated 7/6/2000, leaving the commencement of the Agreement indeterminate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.29}
   ]}
]}

